<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Lichtenberg</h1><p class="st-meta">1816 CE – 1834 CE · capital St. Wendel</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Detached Saar-area exclave of Saxe-Coburg, given at Vienna and sold to Prussia after political unrest.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> promised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-i-duke-of-saxe-coburg-and-gotha">Duke of Saxe-Coburg-Saalfeld</a> territory for his services against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>. In 1816 he received a district around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-wendel">St. Wendel</a> on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahe">Nahe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blies">Blies</a>, far from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a>, which was named the Principality of Lichtenberg in 1819 after the castle near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kusel">Kusel</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The principality had about 25,000 inhabitants, Catholic and Protestant, living from farming and small crafts. It was governed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a> officials who were unpopular, and the region was touched by the radical democratic movement around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hambach-festival">Hambach Festival</a> of 1832. Unrest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-wendel">St. Wendel</a> in 1832 required troops to restore order.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-i-duke-of-saxe-coburg-and-gotha">Ernest I</a> found the distant territory hard to govern and sold it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1834 for an annual payment. It became the district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-wendel">St. Wendel</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine-province">Rhine Province</a> and is now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarland">Saarland</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-lichtenberg.jpg" alt="Principality of Lichtenberg" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: David Liuzzo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flagge_Herzogtum_Sachsen-Coburg-Gotha_(1911-1920).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1816 CE – 1834 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>St. Wendel</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ernest I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Fürstentum Lichtenberg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1825&amp;focus=principality-of-lichtenberg" aria-label="Principality of Lichtenberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4847 450 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Lichtenberg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5167 706L5251 687M5167 706L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5251 687h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5167 706h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5167 706h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1825&amp;focus=principality-of-lichtenberg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
